that amelia created the book herself  like there's a couple scenes where she  has you know what looks like charcoal  like on her hands and things like that  she did model the creature after her  son's like magician get up like with the  hat and the cloak and everything and she  later sort of like personifies the  monster  as she kind of gives into her own hatred  and essentially becomes the monster i'm  kind of of the opinion that the sort of  depth of her hostility toward the kid  brought the monster quite literally like  into the world and the thing about it is  a lot of horror movies kind of play with  this trope so i don't know why people  singled out the babadook and complained  about that like in particular because a  lot of horror movies do that but again  whatever another very common grape uh  that i see with this movie is that  probably the most common gripe i feel  like is that samuel is an annoying  little [ __ ] who you would really like to  drop kick into next week now i will  agree with that that is absolutely true  but again  that's absolutely the point of that  character  at the beginning of the movie you're  actually supposed to sympathize with  amelia  um as she has to deal with this  incorrigible little hell beast that's  like running around her house  um you know i'm not a parent myself but  i've known enough parents that have told  me that there are lots of times when  your kid is like misbehaving that you  actively dislike this child and you feel  kind of like bad about it so i really do  love that this movie  took this very common but also a little  bit taboo emotion that a lot of people  have um and sort of like did sort of  like an exploration of it now so the  viewer is supposed to think that samuel  sucks and to want to strangle the brat  right i mean that's kind of what you're  supposed to feel about him  but then slowly as the story unfolds  you start to realize that samuel is just  a little kid i mean he's just like a  vulnerable little six-year-old kid and  even though he's awful  he's only responding to the way his  mother treats him in the only way that  he knows how to respond because again  he's just a little kid so when amelia  goes kind of like full-on monster toward  the end your sympathies have kind of  like shifted towards samuel as he sort  of like falls into the crosshairs and  you realize you've been sympathizing  with a woman who is gonna strangle her  own child you know what i mean which is  probably not a place you want to be and  samuel actually does become more  sympathetic as the movie goes on because  you you see that he actually does love  his mother and he wants to protect her  but he's only acting that way because of  the way that because of this kind of  monster that's within her that he senses  and he senses that he's in danger from  her and he doesn't really know how to  respond to that other than by like  acting out now i have also seen the  argument made that the kids behavior was  like too over the top i will note though  that i grew up around lots and lots of  kids i don't have any kids my own but  like i said i grew up around lots and  lots of kids like i have several  siblings and tons of cousins and uh my  parents ran a daycare at one point so i  was around a lot of little kids all the  time i've actually been first-hand  witness to a handful of children who  acted absolutely the same way that  samuel does in this movie i kind of feel  like nowadays that would maybe be  diagnosed as maybe adhd or you know  maybe even like some aspects of autism  or something like that so i don't really  think it's over the top in particular  like i said you're supposed to hate the  kids so if it's a little bit over the  top that's why although this film does  seem somewhat divisive like in the  horror community i guess i'm in the camp  of people who really really loved it i  mean i really loved it had just these  arresting visuals like that monster  looked so cool like it was like a paper  puppet and like the way it kind of like  moved around the ceiling and stuff like  so great like so creepy and it just had  this really great like all-around sort  of like eerie vibe like very cool very  shadowy very silent movie-esque i love  that kind of stuff and i really did like  the sort of metaphorical deep dive that  it did into  these complicated feelings that that  some mothers have uh toward their  offspring um the acting in this is also  phenomenal and it really did sell the  escalating horror between amelia and her  son like as the monster got more and  more powerful i mean not everyone is  into this movie and that's okay  but i really think this is easily one of  my favorite horror movies of the past  several years so whether you agree or  disagree let me know in the comments and  we'll have a discussion about it and  that will do it for this flickers of  fear i will see you guys on the next one  bye
 

